 ],
 "literal": "Tinkling anklet removed, on his feet bright hero's anklet having worn, / tuft of hair removed, on his forehead neem's bright sprout / with long-vined uḻiñai foliage having worn as a wreath, / short armlets removed, in his hand bow having grasped, / on the tall chariot's board so as to shine, he who stood, / who indeed is he? May he live long! His chaplet and chest-garland having worn, / his child's amulet he has not even removed! Milk having left / having angered and come against him, the new warriors / firmly having grasped, so that wide sky's roar rises, / even having rejoiced, or even having despised them, he has not done even more than that!",
 "translation": "Who is he, standing so illustriously on the hand-rest of his tall chariot, wearing bright hero's anklets on feet from which the children's tinkling anklets were only recently removed, wearing bright sprouts of neem on his forehead from which the child's tuft was only recently shaved, wreathed with the long vines of uḻiñai, and holding a bow in hands that have only just laid aside children's short bracelets? May he live long! He wears his battle-chaplet and chest-garland, yet he has not even removed his child's protective amulet! He has only just stopped drinking milk. Yet, when new warriors came raging against him, he gripped them so firmly that a great roar rose to the wide sky; he did not rejoice in his victory, nor did he look down upon them, doing nothing more than his duty.",
 "modern": "குழந்தைக்கான கிண்கிணியைக் கழற்றிவிட்டு, கால்களில் ஒளிரும் வீரக்கழலை அணிந்து, குழந்தைக் குடுமியை நீக்கிவிட்டு, நெற்றியில் வேம்பின் ஒளிரும் தளிரையும் நீண்ட கொடியையுடைய உழிஞைப் பூவையும் சூடி, சிறு வளையல்களைக் கழற்றிய கைகளில் வில்லை ஏந்தி, உயர்ந்த தேரின் கொடிஞ்சியில் விளங்குமாறு நிற்பவன் யார்? அவன் வாழ்க! அவன் தன் தலையிலும் மார்பிலும் மாலைகளைச் சூடியிருந்தாலும், இன்னும் தன் கழுத்திலிருந்து குழந்தைக் காப்புத் தாலியைக் கூடக் கழற்றவில்லை. பால் குடிப்பதையும் இப்போதுதான் நிறுத்தியிருக்கிறான். சினந்து தன்மேல் வந்த புதிய பகை வீரர்களை இறுக்கமாகப் பற்றி, அகன்ற வானத்தில் ஆரவாரம் எழும்படி வென்றான். அவ்வாறு வென்றபோதும் அவன் மகிழ்ச்சி அடையவுமில்லை; பகைவர்களை இகழவுமில்லை; அதற்கு மேல் ஒன்றும் செய்யவில்லை.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Vākai tiṇai (victory) and the theme is Aracavākai (the king's victory), composed by Idaikkunṟūr Kiḻār in praise of the young Pāṇṭiyaṉ Talaiyālaṅkāṉattu Ceruveṉṟa Neṭuñceḻiyaṉ. The poet marvels at the extreme youth of the king, who has just transitioned from childhood to kingship and war. He describes how the king has replaced child ornaments like the tinkling anklets (kiṇkiṇi), the tuft of hair (kuṭumi), and short bracelets (kuṟuntoṭi) with warrior gear like the hero's anklet (kaḻal), neem sprouts (vēmpu), and a bow (cāpam). Despite his youth—he has only recently stopped drinking milk and still wears his child's protective amulet (tāli)—he has defeated seasoned enemy warriors who came against him. The poet highlights the young king's maturity, noting that after his resounding victory, he neither exulted in pride nor demeaned his fallen foes.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
